†Code L120:   * cite63†Issues2 (2) L121:   * cite64†Pull requests L122:   * cite65†Actions L123:   * cite66†Projects L124:   * cite67†Security and quality L125:   * cite68†Insights L126: \nL127: More items\nL128: \nL129: cite69†Image: wangjiangshan0725†avatars.githubusercontent.com L130: \nL131: cite62†Elastic-DiT L132: \nL133: Public\nL134: \nL135:   * cite70†Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL136:   * cite70†Fork0 (0) L137:   * cite70†Star50 (50) You must be signed in to star a repository\nL138: ## About\nL139: \nL140: [ICML 2026] Elastic Diffusion Transformer: Accelerating SOTA generation models (e.g., Qwen-Image, Hunyuan3d ) through adaptive computation\nL141: \nL142: ### Topics\nL143: \nL144: cite71†acceleration cite72†hunyuan3d cite73†lightweight cite74†qwen-image L145: \nL146: ### Resources\nL147: \nL148: cite75†Readme L149: \nL150: cite76†Activity L151: \nL152: ### Stars\nL153: \nL154: 50 stars\nL155: \nL156: ### Watchers\nL157: \nL158: 3 watching\nL159: \nL160: ### Forks\nL161: \nL162: cite77†0 forks L163: \nL164: cite78†Report repository L165: \nL166: main\nL167: \nL168: cite79†Branches cite80†Tags L169: \nL170: [Input: Go to file]\nL171: \nL172: Go to file\nL173: \nL174: Code\nL175: \nL176: Open more actions menu\nL177: \nL178: ## Latest commit\nL179: \nL180: \nL181: \nL182: ## History\nL183: \nL184: 5 Commits\nL185: ## Folders and files\nL186: \nL187: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL188: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL189: cite81†assets/repo_figures | cite81†assets/repo_figures |    |\nL190: cite82†diffsynth | cite82†diffsynth |    |\nL191: cite83†.gitignore | cite83†.gitignore |    |\nL192: cite84†README.md | cite84†README.md |    |\nL193: cite85†infer.py | cite85†infer.py |    |\nL194: cite86†pyproject.toml | cite86†pyproject.toml |    |\nL195: cite87†results.jpg | cite87†results.jpg |    |\nL196: \nL197: ## Repository files navigation\nL198: \nL199:   *   * cite88†README L200: \nL201: More items\nL202: # Elastic Diffusion Transformer\nL203: \nL204: cite89†Jiangshan Wang†scholar.google.com ^{1,2,3}, cite90†Zeqiang Lai†zeqiang-lai.github.io ^{1,2†}, cite91†Jiarui Chen†scholar.google.com ^{2,4}, cite92†Jiayi Guo†www.jiayiguo.net ^{3},\nL205: cite93†Hang Guo†csguoh.github.io ^{3}, cite94†Xiu Li†scholar.google.com ^{3}, cite95†Xiangyu Yue†xyue.io ^{1*}, cite96†Chunchao Guo†dblp.org ^{2*}\nL206: \nL207: ^{1} CUHK MMLab, ^{2} Tencent Hunyuan, ^{3} Tsinghua University, ^{4} HITSZ\nL208: \nL209: ^{†} Project Lead ^{*} Corresponding Author\nL210: Diffusion Transformers (DiT) have demonstrated remarkable generative capabilities but remain highly computationally expensive. Previous acceleration methods, such as pruning and distillation, typically rely on a fixed computational capacity, leading to insufficient acceleration and degraded generation quality. To address this limitation, we propose Elastic Diffusion Transformer (E-DiT) , an adaptive acceleration framework for DiT that effectively improves efficiency while maintaining generation quality.\nL211: Specifically, we observe that the generative process of DiT exhibits substantial sparsity (i.e., some computations can be skipped with minimal impact on quality), and this sparsity varies significantly across samples. Motivated by this observation, E-DiT equips each DiT block with a lightweight router that dynamically identifies sample-dependent sparsity from the input latent. Each router adaptively determines whether the corresponding block can be skipped.\nL212: If the block is not skipped, the router then predicts the optimal MLP width reduction ratio within the block. During inference, we further introduce a block-level feature caching mechanism that leverages router predictions to eliminate redundant computations in a training-free manner. Extensive experiments across 2D image (Qwen-Image and FLUX) and 3D asset (Hunyuan3D-3.0) demonstrate the effectiveness of E-DiT, achieving up to ∼2× speedup with negligible loss in generation quality.\nL213: # 🔥 News\nL214: \nL215:   * [2026.5.1] 🎉Our paper is accepted by ICML 2026!\nL216:   * [2026.2.25] Inference code for image generation is released!\nL217:   * [2026.2.15] Paper released!\nL218: \nL219: # 💻 Code\nL220: ## 🛠️ Setup\nL221: \nL222:   * Running the following command to construct the environment. We use the DiffSynth-Studio as the codebase to develop E-DiT, you can also refer to their cite97†official repo if there is any problem about the environment.\nL223: \nL224:     `git clone https://github.com/wangjiangshan0725/Elastic-DiT.git\nL225:     cd Elastic-DiT\nL226:     pip install -e .\nL227:     `\nL228: \nL229:   * Download the weight of Qwen-Image from their official cite98†Huggingface Repo†huggingface.co and put them at ckpt/Qwen-Image;\nL230:   * Download the weight of E-DiT cite99†here†huggingface.co and put it at ckpt/model.safetensors\nL231: ## 🚀 Inference\nL232: \nL233:     `python infer.py\nL234:     `\nL235: \nL236: # 🎨 Gallery\nL237: \nL238: ## Image Generation\nL239: \nL240: ## 3D Asset Generation\nL241: # 🖋️ Citation\nL242: \nL243: If you find our work helpful, please star 🌟 this repo and cite 📑 our paper. Thanks for your support!\nL244: \nL245:     `@article{wang2026elastic,\nL246:       title={Elastic Diffusion Transformer},\nL247:       author={Wang, Jiangshan and Lai, Zeqiang and Chen, Jiarui and Guo, Jiayi and Guo, Hang and Li, Xiu and Yue, Xiangyu and Guo, Chunchao},\nL248:       journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.13993},\nL249:       year={2026}\nL250:     }\nL251:     `\nL252: # Acknowledgements\nL253: \nL254: We thank cite100†Qwen-Image and cite101†DiffSynth-Studio for their clean codebase.\nL255: \nL256: # Contact\nL257: \nL258: The code in this repository is still being reorganized. Errors that may arise during the organizing process could lead to code malfunctions or discrepancies from the original research results. If you have any questions or concerns, please send emails to wjs23@mails.tsinghua.edu.cn.\nL259: ## About\nL260: \nL261: [ICML 2026] Elastic Diffusion Transformer: Accelerating SOTA generation models (e.g., Qwen-Image, Hunyuan3d ) through adaptive computation\nL262: \nL263: ### Topics\nL264: \nL265: cite71†acceleration cite72†hunyuan3d cite73†lightweight cite74†qwen-image L266: \nL267: ### Resources\nL268: \nL269: cite75†Readme L270: \nL271: cite76†Activity L272: \nL273: ### Stars\nL274: \nL275: 50 stars\nL276: \nL277: ### Watchers\nL278: \nL279: 3 watching\nL280: \nL281: ### Forks\nL282: \nL283: cite77†0 forks L284: \nL285: cite78†Report repository L286: \nL287: ## Releases\nL288: \nL289: ## Packages\nL290: \nL291: ## Contributors\nL292: \nL293: ## Languages\nL294: \nL295: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nREDSearcher: A Scalable and Cost-Efficient Framework for Long-Horizon Search Agents (https://redsearchagent.github.io/index/)\nciteturn31896view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn31894view1\",\"id\":21}); Total lines: 83\nL0: cite0†Image: REDSearcher Logo L1: # REDSearcher: A Scalable and Cost-Efficient\nL2: Framework for Long-Horizon Search Agents\nL3: \nL4: cite1†Zheng Chu ^{1*}, cite1†Xiao Wang ^{2*†✉}, cite1†Jack Hong ^{2*}, cite1†Huiming Fan ^{1}, cite1†Yuqi Huang ^{3}, cite1†Yue Yang ^{3}, cite1†Guohai Xu ^{2}, cite1†Shengchao Hu ^{3}, cite1†Dongdong Kuang ^{2}, cite1†Chenxiao Zhao ^{2}, cite1†Cheng Xiang ^{2}, cite1†Ming Liu ^{1✉}, cite1†Bing Qin ^{1}, cite1†Xing Yu ^{2}\nL5: \nL6: ^{1}Harbin Institute of Technology, ^{2}Xiaohongshu, ^{3}Shanghai Jiao Tong University\nL7: ^{*}Equal Contribution ^{†}Project Leader ^{✉}Corresponding Author\nL8: \nL9: cite2†Paper†huggingface.co cite3†Code†github.com cite4†Image: HF†huggingface.co Model (Coming Soon)\nL10: \nL11: cite5†SFT Dataset (Text)†huggingface.co cite6†RL Dataset Demo (Text)†huggingface.co cite7†SFT Dataset (MM)†huggingface.co L12: \nL13: 🛠 Scalable task synthesis via graph-structured reasoning with topological complexity control\nL14: \nL15: 🚀 Cost-efficient training via mid-training of core search-agent subskills\nL16: 🏆 SOTA performance across both text-only and multimodal benchmarks\nL17: \nL18: cite8†Image: REDSearcher Results L19: ## Our Pipeline\nL20: \nL21: cite9†Image: REDSearcher Pipeline L22: Figure 2. QA Generation. We construct directed acyclic graphs (DAGs) from Knowledge-Graph entities and Web-Walk hyperlinks, enabling explicit difficulty control via topological complexity. Each node is enriched with multi-source evidence, then sampled to form reasoning paths with Query Fuzzing (entity/attribute anonymization) to increase search challenge. Verifier Pipeline.\nL23: A cascaded filter progressively validates quality: LLM difficulty check, QA-graph alignment, Google retrieval verification, hallucination detection, agent rollout confirmation, and answer uniqueness validation—producing 20K+ verified trajectories at >85% accuracy.\nL24: ## Performance\nL25: \nL26: Comparison between REDSearcher and closed / open agentic models\nL27: Model  | BrowseComp  | BrowseComp-zh  | GAIA  | HLE  | Overall\nL28: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL29: Proprietary Deep Research Agents\nL30: Seed–1.8  | 67.6  | 81.3  | 87.4  | 40.9  | 69.3\nL31: Gemini–2.5–pro–DR  | 7.6  | 27.3  | -  | -  | -\nL32: Gemini–3–Pro  | 37.8  | 51.6  | 74.8  | 45.8  | 52.5\nL33: Claude–4.5–sonnet  | 24.1  | 42.4  | 66.0  | 32.0  | 41.1\nL34: OpenAI–o3  | 49.7  | 58.1  | 70.5  | 20.2  | 49.6\nL35: GPT–5–Thinking–high  | 54.9  | 63.0  | 76.7  | 41.7  | 59.1\nL36: GPT–5.2–Thinking–xhigh  | 65.8  | 76.1  | -  | -  | -\nL37: Open-source Deep Research Agents\nL38: Kimi–K2.5–Agent  | 60.6 / 74.9*  | 